WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.016 --> 00:00:04.801
The Italian Renaissance was a period of extraordinary growth and culture.

2
00:00:04.802 --> 00:00:13.232
Renaissance means rebirth, and European culture, society, and art were reborn and transformed during this age.

3
00:00:13.233 --> 00:00:19.223
Sculptors, artists, musicians, and writers began to dramatically shape the world around them.

4
00:00:19.224 --> 00:00:31.159
Amazing works of art by some of the world’s greatest artists, such as Leonardo da Vinci and Michelangelo, were produced, and new philosophical ideas such as humanism became popular.

5
00:00:31.160 --> 00:00:36.537
At the center of the Italian Renaissance was the Medici family and the city of Florence.

6
00:00:36.538 --> 00:00:43.077
In the late fourteenth century, Italy was a divided country and each city was a state within itself.

7
00:00:43.078 --> 00:00:51.800
In Florence, one of the largest and most important cities, many members of the Medici family held public office and were well-known city officials.

8
00:00:51.801 --> 00:00:57.363
But the most powerful members of the Medici family ruled from behind the scenes.

9
00:00:57.364 --> 00:01:04.415
With their vast amounts of money and strong influence, the Medici family manipulated and directed politics and culture.

10
00:01:04.416 --> 00:01:14.449
Although Florence’s government appeared to be a republic, meaning a state ruled by representatives chosen by the people, it was, in fact, more of a monarchy.

11
00:01:14.450 --> 00:01:22.172
The first Medici to truly rule the city was Cosimo the Elder, who lived from 1389 to 1464.

12
00:01:22.173 --> 00:01:34.287
Together with the Roman Catholic Church, the Medici family founded a successful banking company, which collected money from people throughout Italy and much of Europe, so the Medici became extremely wealthy.

13
00:01:34.288 --> 00:01:42.653
Even though they were members of the patrician class&#8212;a high social class&#8212;the Medici family worked hard to aid the middle and lower classes.

14
00:01:42.654 --> 00:01:47.312
In return, the family enjoyed broad support from the people of Florence.

15
00:01:47.313 --> 00:01:57.647
Cosimo the Elder, in particular, was a favorite of the working and lower classes, since he lived humbly and spent most of his great wealth on projects to improve the city.

16
00:01:57.648 --> 00:02:05.421
His popularity had much to do with his deep belief in humanism and his commitment to art, culture, and education.

17
00:02:05.422 --> 00:02:18.684
Humanism was an idea that emerged during the Renaissance and soon found widespread acceptance; this philosophy acknowledged that God created humankind, but celebrated the role of humans in creating civilization.

18
00:02:18.685 --> 00:02:27.770
This concept was also closely tied to the belief that art, literature, and science&#8212;indeed, all culture&#8212;made human beings deeply valuable.

19
00:02:27.771 --> 00:02:40.813
Human worth and value were praised and artistic skill was highly respected, so possessing talent for the fine arts, architecture, or literature could lead a person to great achievements in society.

20
00:02:40.814 --> 00:02:54.328
Influenced by these humanist ideals, Cosimo publicly invited scholars and writers to his palace and encouraged them to explore new insights into society and the human condition.

21
00:02:54.329 --> 00:03:08.575
Cosimo also believed that everyone should have access to education and the arts, so he helped create hundreds of Christian charities, schools, and churches, in addition to supporting the creation of performance spaces for poor artists.

22
00:03:08.576 --> 00:03:14.042
As a result of Cosimo’s hard work and beliefs, Florence thrived in business and culture.

23
00:03:14.043 --> 00:03:25.311
He created the largest public library in Europe, which held some of the most important works of history, including ancient Greek and Roman texts, many of which are still widely read today.

24
00:03:25.312 --> 00:03:31.467
He also created an academy where ancient and modern texts were studied by the best minds in Italy.

25
00:03:31.468 --> 00:03:43.934
In addition to his public contributions, Cosimo personally supported the arts and was a patron of some of the best-known Renaissance artists and sculptors, such as Donatello and Brunelleschi.

26
00:03:43.935 --> 00:03:54.492
Under his leadership, Florence became the cultural center of Italy and much of Europe, a truly great city of the golden age of the Renaissance.

27
00:03:54.493 --> 00:04:05.112
The second influential member of the Medici family was Lorenzo the Magnificent, Cosimo the Elder’s grandson, who lived from 1449 to 1492.

28
00:04:05.113 --> 00:04:14.867
Like his grandfather and many of the other Medici, Lorenzo ruled Florence unofficially from behind the scenes and was popular with the middle and working classes.

29
00:04:14.868 --> 00:04:19.262
He too was a humanist and very devoted to education and the arts.

30
00:04:19.263 --> 00:04:31.259
But, although Lorenzo’s power was still supported by the tremendous Medici wealth, he was not the astute businessman that his grandfather had been, and Florence’s economy suffered.

31
00:04:31.260 --> 00:04:36.624
His bad banking decisions actually led the Medici family to lose some of their fortune.

32
00:04:36.625 --> 00:04:47.411
Lorenzo was also not as politically gifted as Cosimo, and relations between the Pope and Florence worsened during his rule, finally resulting in a war.

33
00:04:47.412 --> 00:04:57.063
But even though Lorenzo’s decisions did not always lead to greater economic and political success for the city, Florence remained a bustling center of trade and culture.

34
00:04:57.064 --> 00:05:05.994
Trade in luxury items such as spices and fine fabrics flourished, and Florence, already powerful after Cosimo’s rule, remained strong.

35
00:05:05.995 --> 00:05:10.967
Most importantly, Lorenzo the Magnificent was a major patron of the arts.

36
00:05:10.968 --> 00:05:18.815
In his 23-year reign over Florence, the Renaissance reached its peak and the city experienced a golden age.

37
00:05:18.816 --> 00:05:28.253
The Florence court had the greatest number of master artists of any place in the world, and the city saw a tremendous amount of construction and activity.

38
00:05:28.254 --> 00:05:36.908
The center of Lorenzo’s court was the Palazzo Medici or Medici Palace, which had been built by Cosimo and renovated by Lorenzo.

39
00:05:36.909 --> 00:05:45.852
In the 1470s and 1480s, this magnificently decorated palace was home to some of the greatest artists and philosophers in Italy.

40
00:05:45.853 --> 00:05:52.719
Lorenzo supported the arts by commissioning works from new artists and then allowing them to live in the palace.

41
00:05:52.720 --> 00:05:57.568
Most importantly, he introduced their art to Florence and made them famous.

42
00:05:57.569 --> 00:06:11.615
Lorenzo loved to surround himself with artists; he himself was also an accomplished poet, writer, and painter&#8212;what we would call today "a Renaissance man," a person with abilities in many areas.

43
00:06:11.616 --> 00:06:14.729
His poetry, in particular, was highly praised.

44
00:06:14.730 --> 00:06:24.985
He was a patron to many great men, such as Michelangelo and Leonardo da Vinci, both sculptors and painters, and Machiavelli, an influential politician and writer.

45
00:06:24.986 --> 00:06:32.364
All these men became influential artists and thinkers who helped create a name for Florence during the Renaissance.

46
00:06:32.365 --> 00:06:44.523
Throughout these years, Lorenzo maintained the image of the Medici as one of the great families in Italy, and famous politicians, intellectuals, and artists were always visiting the Medici Palace.

47
00:06:44.524 --> 00:06:50.901
Lorenzo was considered a strong ruler who truly believed in the ideals of the Renaissance.

48
00:06:50.902 --> 00:07:00.061
He held total power in the city and brought prestige and greatness to Florence, which is why he is known as Lorenzo the Magnificent.

49
00:07:00.062 --> 00:07:08.047
When Lorenzo the Magnificent died in 1492, Medici power in Florence weakened for a short period under the rule of his sons.

50
00:07:08.048 --> 00:07:14.380
Later, however, members of the family rose again to powerful positions, such as cardinal and even pope.

51
00:07:14.381 --> 00:07:21.592
Until the seventeenth century, the Medici family remained one of the wealthiest and most powerful families in Florence.

52
00:07:21.593 --> 00:07:28.964
But Cosimo the Elder and his grandson, Lorenzo the Magnificent, remain the most famous of the Medici rulers.

53
00:07:28.965 --> 00:07:38.556
Their generous sponsorship of the arts and their commitment to education and humanistic ideals are an inspiration to wealthy patrons around the world today.

